<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mobina Kashaniyan and Ali Jannesari
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
Department of Computer Science
<br class="ltx_break">Iowa State University, USA 
<br class="ltx_break">{mobina, jannesar}@iastate.edu

</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Test-time scaling (TTS) improves the reasoning of large language models (LLMs) by generating several samples for a query and voting on the answer.
However, a fixed sampling budget ignores two facts: different queries need different amounts of reasoning, and the cost of one more sample changes with GPU load.
In our measurements, the same four-sample query uses up to 4.1<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> more energy when other jobs load the GPU.
Existing adaptive methods mainly set the budget from answer agreement, question structure, or question difficulty.
They decide how much reasoning a query needs, but not what that reasoning costs on the GPU right now.
We present <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>, a runtime framework for resource-aware TTS with two modes.
Its default mode, <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online, needs no offline calibration: it starts with one sample, checks whether the plurality answer is stable, reads the live GPU state, predicts the cost of the next step with an online cost model, and decides whether to stop or continue.
For stable deployments that can reuse a one-time profile, <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> also offers an offline mode, <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated, which picks the budget from accuracy and cost models built offline.
We evaluate <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> with Phi-3-mini on an NVIDIA A100 across four reasoning and question-answering datasets under a controlled idle-to-high GPU load schedule, and repeat the study on an NVIDIA L40S and with a second model, Qwen2.5-3B.
On the A100, <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online answers every query, never exceeds the energy budget, gives the highest or statistically tied accuracy on all four datasets, and uses fewer samples as load rises.
The same pattern holds on the L40S and with Qwen2.5-3B, where <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online again answers every query within the budget.
On GSM8K at high load, it keeps 60.0% accuracy, while <span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated drops to 44.0% because it defers 42.4% of the queries.
A controlled ablation shows that online cost learning is what makes the budget follow the load. Including offline calibration, <span id="abstract1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online uses 42<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> less GPU time and 48<math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> less energy than <span id="abstract1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated.
Its main costs are higher tail latency and lower throughput on short tasks, and offline calibration remains strong only when the load is stable.
These results show that TTS on shared GPUs is a runtime resource-management problem, not only an accuracy problem.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>large language models, test-time scaling, GPU load, resource-aware inference, adaptive sampling, energy-aware systems

</div>
