tx_Math" alttext="\Delta U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta U</annotation></semantics></math> is how much the next step raises this; \scriptsize2⃝ the online cost model learns from the measured energy of every step, follows the load, and predicts the next step’s energy with a safety margin; \scriptsize3⃝ with the same votes, <span id="S0.F2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online continues on an idle GPU but stops on a busy one.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) give better answers when they use more computation at inference time.
A simple and popular way to do this is <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">test-time scaling</em> (TTS) with voting: the model generates several answers (samples) for the same question, and the system returns the most common one <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.
More samples usually mean better answers, but they also mean more GPU work, more energy, and longer waiting time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Because of this cost, recent work makes TTS <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adaptive</em>.
These methods give more samples to hard queries and fewer to easy ones, using signals such as answer agreement, query difficulty, or reasoning progress <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
They answer one question well: <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how many samples does this query need?</em>
However, they treat the cost of one sample as a fixed number. On a real system, this cost is not fixed.
LLM performance depends on batching, scheduling, and memory management <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, and the same number of samples can have very different energy, latency, and throughput costs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Many GPUs in clusters and HPC centers are <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shared</em>: other jobs run on the same GPU at the same time and compete with the LLM for compute, memory bandwidth, and power, and this competition changes over time.
In our measurements on an A100, the same four-sample query uses up to 4.1<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> more energy when the GPU is heavily loaded (Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III Motivation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
For example, a GSM8K query costs 121.7 J on an idle GPU and 455.6 J on a busy one.
A controller that ignores this change either wastes energy when the GPU is busy, or uses up its energy budget too early and must <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">defer</em> later queries, which means refusing to answer them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">So a TTS controller on a shared GPU must answer two questions at the same time:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Is one more sample useful for this query?</em></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">What does that sample cost on the GPU right now?</em></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We present <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>, a resource-aware TTS framework that answers both questions.
Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the idea: when the GPU is busy, <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> skips samples that are not worth their higher cost.
<span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> has two modes that share the same budget ladder, GPU monitor, and energy accounting (Fig. <a href="#S0.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Its default mode, <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST<span id="S1.p5.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Online</span></span>, needs no offline profiling.
It works as a closed control loop: it generates samples in small steps, checks whether the vote is becoming stable, reads the live GPU state, measures what each step really cost, updates an online cost model, and stops as soon as another step is not worth its cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Some deployments, however, run the same model and workload on a GPU whose load rarely changes, or need low tail latency.
For these cases <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> also offers an offline mode, <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST<span id="S1.p6.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Calibrated</span></span>, which profiles each dataset once and picks the whole budget per query from accuracy and cost models, together with the live GPU state.
Choosing <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> once lets it generate all samples in one batch, which gives lower tail latency and higher throughput on short tasks.
The price is the profile itself, which took 5.73 GPU-hours and 7.16 MJ for our four datasets, and a cost model that does not follow the GPU when the load changes.
Including profiling, <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online uses 42<math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> less GPU time and 48<math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> less energy than <span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated on the same 2,000 queries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This difference matters most when the GPU becomes busy.
On GSM8K under high load, <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated must defer 42.4% of the queries, so it answers only 44.0% of them correctly.
Under the same load, <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online answers every query, keeps 60.0% accuracy, and uses less energy per query (175.8 J vs. 206.4 J).
Across all four datasets, <span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online never defers a query, never exceeds the energy budget, and gives the highest or tied-best accuracy. We also test <em id="S1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">why</em> <span id="S1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online changes its budget.
In a controlled ablation, we keep the remaining-energy signal fixed, so that budget depletion cannot drive the decision.
Without online cost learning, the controller uses the same mean budget (<math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=3.12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=3.12</annotation></semantics></math>) at every load level on all four datasets.
With online cost learning, the budget follows the load.
So the adaptation comes from the runtime resource signals, not from the budget running out.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span>
This paper makes three contributions:</p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show with measurements that the cost of a TTS sample is not fixed: on a GPU shared with other jobs, the same four-sample query uses up to 4.1<math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> more energy under high load. Based on this, we treat adaptive TTS as a runtime resource-management problem, where each decision weighs the value of more reasoning against its current cost.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> with two modes. Its default mode, <span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online, learns the cost of sampling online and decides step by step, with no dataset-specific profiling. Its offline mode, <span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated, picks the budget once from offline accuracy and cost models together with the live GPU state, for deployments that can reuse a one-time profile.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show when each mode should be used. Under changing load, <span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online answers every query and stays within the energy budget, while <span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated defers up to 42.4% of the queries. Under stable load, offline calibration remains a strong choice. A controlled ablation shows that online cost learning is what makes the budget follow the load.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Background and Related Work</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Test-Time Scaling and Adaptive Sampling</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Test-time scaling (TTS) uses more computation at inference time to get better answers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
A common form is to generate several answers for the same query and combine them.
Chain-of-thought prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> lets the model write out its reasoning, and self-consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> samples several reasoning paths and returns the answer that appears most often.
We call the number of samples the <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sampling budget</em> <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. The same budget is not useful for every query.
Easy questions are often answered correctly after one or two samples, while hard questions may need more.
Adaptive methods therefore change <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> per query.
Adaptive-Consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and Early-Stopping Self-Consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> stop sampling once the answers agree.
Other methods use question difficulty, answer confidence, or reasoning progress <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These methods remove many unnecessary samples.
However, they decide only from the reasoning side: they ask whether more samples would change the answer, but not what those samples cost on the GPU right now.
Recent work shows that the number of samples alone does not describe the systems cost of TTS, because the same samples can have very different energy, latency, and throughput costs depending on how they run <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
<span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> adds this missing systems side to adaptive TTS.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GPU-Aware and Energy-Efficient LLM Serving</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM serving systems<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> show that runtime conditions strongly affect inference performance.
Orca <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> improve batching and GPU memory management.
Llumnix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> moves requests between model instances at runtime to balance load and reduce latency, and Bullet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> changes GPU resource allocation during execution based on current performance conditions. Energy is also a main goal in LLM serving.
Samsi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> measured the energy cost of LLM inference on V100 and A100 GPUs.
POLCA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> studies power management and power oversubscription in LLM clusters, and DynamoLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> reconfigures inference clusters to save energy while meeting latency targets.
throttLL’eM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> adjusts GPU frequency and resource configuration to reduce inference energy while meeting latency targets.
EnerTune <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> studies energy-aware GPU sharing, models how GPU allocation, frequency, and batch size affect power and performance, and shows that large profiling spaces can make offline profiling expensive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These systems decide <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> to run a given amount of work: where to place requests, how to batch them, and how to set the hardware.
They do not decide <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how much</em> reasoning work each query should get.
<span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> makes this second decision, so it complements these systems and can run on top of them.</p>
</div>
</section>
