<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A simple example.</span>
The following case is only an illustration of the rule.
Suppose a math question has three samples, and all three give the answer 42.
The vote is already stable, so <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta U</annotation></semantics></math> is close to zero and <span id="S5.SS2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online stops at <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=3</annotation></semantics></math>, whether the GPU is idle or busy.
Now suppose the three samples give 42, 42, and 40.
More samples could still change the winner, so <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta U</annotation></semantics></math> is large.
If the GPU is idle, the next step is cheap, <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta U/E_{\mathrm{inc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>inc</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta U/E_{\mathrm{inc}}</annotation></semantics></math> passes the threshold, and <span id="S5.SS2.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online goes to <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=4</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the GPU is busy, the same step costs several times more joules (Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III Motivation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a)), the ratio can drop below <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, and <span id="S5.SS2.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online returns 42.
In both cases the decision uses the same two questions: is the next sample useful, and what does it cost now?</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Two feedback loops.</span>
Each executed step feeds back twice.
The new answers update the reasoning signal <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta U</annotation></semantics></math>, and the measured energy updates the cost model.
So the next decision depends on both how the reasoning is going and how the GPU is behaving now.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overhead.</span>
The controller runs on the host CPU.
Per step, it reads NVML, runs a small Monte Carlo estimate, and does one RLS update.
The main runtime cost is not this computation, but the fact that samples are generated in several smaller steps instead of one batch (§<a href="#S7.SS5" title="VII-E Q5: Runtime Overhead and Cost-Model Error ‣ VII Evaluation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII-E</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST<span id="S5.SS3.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-Calibrated</span></span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated is the offline mode of <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>. We call it <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Calibrated</em> because its accuracy and cost models come from an offline calibration (profiling) phase; it still serves queries at runtime and reads the live GPU state, but its models stay fixed.
For each dataset, we run 500 calibration prompts at each of the five budgets and four load levels (20 settings).
Since the five budgets together use <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+3+4+6+8=22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+3+4+6+8=22</annotation></semantics></math> samples per prompt, this generates <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500\times 22\times 4=44{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>500</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>22</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>44,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500\times 22\times 4=44{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> samples per dataset.
The calibration prompts come from a separate split and do not overlap with the evaluation queries.
From these runs we fit two models.
The <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">accuracy model</em> <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}(q,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}(q,B)</annotation></semantics></math> estimates the chance that budget <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> gives a correct answer for query <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, using query features such as estimated difficulty.
The <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cost model</em> <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{E}(B,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{E}(B,H)</annotation></semantics></math> predicts the energy of budget <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> under GPU condition <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> by predicting its power and run time and multiplying them, since energy is power times time (watts <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> seconds <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math> joules).
Both models stay fixed after this phase.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Online phase.</span>
For each query, <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated reads the current GPU state <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For an upgrade from budget <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> to the next budget <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, it computes the expected accuracy gain and the extra energy:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\hat{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\hat{P}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hat{P}(q,B^{\prime})-\hat{P}(q,B)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hat{P}(q,B^{\prime})-\hat{P}(q,B),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\hat{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\hat{E}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hat{E}(B^{\prime},H_{t})-\hat{E}(B,H_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hat{E}(B^{\prime},H_{t})-\hat{E}(B,H_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The score <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\hat{P}/\Delta\hat{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\hat{P}/\Delta\hat{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is the expected gain per extra joule.
The allocator first gives the query the smallest budget.
Then, among the upgrades on the ladder <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1\!\to\!3\!\to\!4\!\to\!6\!\to\!8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.484em;" width="0.484em"><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.484em;" width="0.484em"><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.484em;" width="0.484em"><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\!\to\!3\!\to\!4\!\to\!6\!\to\!8</annotation></semantics></math> that still fit in the remaining energy, it takes the one with the best score.
It stops when no useful upgrade fits.
If even <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=1</annotation></semantics></math> does not fit according to the cost model, the query is deferred.
After this, all <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> samples are generated and the plurality answer is returned.
The generated samples do not change the chosen budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Weakness.</span>
<span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated uses the live GPU state, but its cost model was learned from the profiling runs and is never updated.
When the real cost moves away from the model, the allocator either overspends early or rejects queries that it could have served.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Methodology</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> under controlled GPU load.
Our goal is to measure both answer quality and system behavior as the GPU condition changes.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model, Hardware, and Settings</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> in bfloat16 on one NVIDIA A100-SXM4-80GB GPU with a 500 W power limit.
We use tensor parallel size 1 and batch size 32.
Sampling uses temperature 0.7, top-<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> 0.95, at most 512 new tokens, and a fixed seed (42).
All methods use the same model, prompts, generation settings, and budget set <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=\{1,3,4,6,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=\{1,3,4,6,8\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To test generalization (§<a href="#S7.SS7" title="VII-G Q7: Does the Behavior Hold on Another GPU and Another Model? ‣ VII Evaluation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII-G</span></a>), we repeat the study on a second GPU type, an NVIDIA L40S with a 350 W power limit, and with a second model, <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, using the same precision (bfloat16), batch size, sampling settings, query sets, and load schedule.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Datasets</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use four datasets with different answer lengths: two math datasets with long answers, GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> and MATH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, and two multiple-choice datasets with short answers, ARC-Challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> and CommonsenseQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.
Each dataset has a development set and a test set of 500 queries each, which do not overlap; all reported results use the test sets. We report accuracy. All query sets and controller settings were frozen before we looked at the results, and we did no tuning afterwards.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span> </span><span id="S6.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Compared Methods</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fixed-4:</span> standard self-consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> with a fixed budget: every answered query gets <math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=4</annotation></semantics></math> samples, and the plurality answer is returned. We use <math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=4</annotation></semantics></math> because it is the reference budget that defines <math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive-Cal:</span> adaptive budget allocation with offline calibration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. It chooses <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> per query from offline accuracy and cost models, but does not use the live GPU state.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST<span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Calibrated</span></span> (§<a href="#S5.SS3" title="V-C ATEST-Calibrated ‣ V ATESTDesign ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-C</span></a>): the offline mode of <span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST<span id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Online</span></span> (§<a href="#S5.SS2" title="V-B ATEST-Online ‣ V ATESTDesign ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B</span></a>): the default mode of <span id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Choice of baselines.</span>
Fixed-budget self-consistency is the base method that all self-consistency methods in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ II-C Research Gap ‣ II Background and Related Work ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> build on, and Adaptive-Cal is the closest prior work, as the only one that models energy.
The other adaptive methods stop sampling from reasoning signals alone, so their budget cannot react to GPU load; our ReasoningOnly-Strict ablation, which uses only such a signal, keeps the same budget at every load level (§<a href="#S7.SS4" title="VII-D Q4: Is the Adaptation Caused by Resource Signals? ‣ VII Evaluation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII-D</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">VI-D</span> </span><span id="S6.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Controlled GPU Load</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To create a shared-GPU setting, we run a background load injector on the same GPU. It competes with the LLM for GPU resources, like other jobs on a shared GPU. The injector is a background thread that runs dense FP16 matrix multiplies on the same GPU for a duty fraction of every 0.2 s period and sleeps for the rest.
We use four levels: <em id="S6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">idle</em> (no injector), <em id="S6.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">low</em>, <em id="S6.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">medium</em>, and <em id="S6.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">high</em>, with injector duty settings of 0.25, 0.55, and 0.85. The duty setting controls how hard the injector works; it is not a utilization target.
We check the real effect with NVML <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.
For example, in the Fixed-4 runs the measured GPU utilization is about 0%, 24%, 54%, and 85%, and the average GPU power on GSM8K rises from 77 W at idle to 431 W at high load. The 500 queries of each run are split equally: 125 queries per load level, in the order idle, low, medium, high.
The schedule is fixed by a hash and is reused exactly for every method.
So all methods see the same queries in the same order under the same injector settings.</p>
</div>
