<section id="S6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">VI-E</span> </span><span id="S6.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Energy Accounting and Budget</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We measure energy by GPU occupancy (<math id="S6.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\mathrm{occ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>occ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\mathrm{occ}}</annotation></semantics></math>). Each microbatch is charged for all the GPU energy used while it runs, computed from NVML power readings and its run time.
When other jobs share the GPU, a microbatch runs longer and the GPU draws more power, so its <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\mathrm{occ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>occ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\mathrm{occ}}</annotation></semantics></math> grows.
We use this accounting because a job on a shared GPU holds the device for its whole run time, so this is the energy it is responsible for; the same accounting is used for all methods, so it does not favor any of them.
Each run gets one shared energy budget <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, set from the reference energy needed to run <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=4</annotation></semantics></math> under the reference GPU condition. We set this budget separately for each GPU, model, and dataset, because the cost of a sample depends on all three.
All methods get the same budget.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">VI-F</span> </span><span id="S6.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Metrics</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quality and coverage.</span>
<em id="S6.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Accuracy</em> is computed over <em id="S6.SS6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> 500 queries; a deferred query counts as wrong.
<em id="S6.SS6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Defer</em> is the percentage of deferred queries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sampling effort.</span>
<em id="S6.SS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mean <math id="S6.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math></em> is the average budget.
In overall results we count a deferred query as <math id="S6.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=0</annotation></semantics></math>; in per-load results we average only over answered queries, so that the budget choice is visible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Energy.</span>
We report <math id="S6.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\mathrm{occ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>occ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\mathrm{occ}}</annotation></semantics></math> per query and <em id="S6.SS6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">joules per correct answer</em>:</p>
<table id="S6.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{J/correct}=\frac{\text{total }E_{\mathrm{occ}}}{\text{number of correct answers}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>J/correct</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>total </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>occ</mi></msub></mrow><mtext>number of correct answers</mtext></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{J/correct}=\frac{\text{total }E_{\mathrm{occ}}}{\text{number of correct answers}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This metric gets worse both when a method spends energy on samples that do not help and when it gets fewer correct answers, for example by deferring queries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Budget safety.</span>
<em id="S6.SS6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Budget used</em> is total energy divided by <math id="S6.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A value above 100% means the method <em id="S6.SS6.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">overshot</em> the budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">System performance.</span>
We report 95th-percentile (p95) latency per query, query throughput (queries per second, QPS), GPU utilization, and power.
For <span id="S6.SS6.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online we also report the error of its online cost model as mean absolute percentage error (MAPE).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS7.6" class="ltx_text">VI-G</span> </span><span id="S6.SS7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Calibration Cost</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We measure the offline profiling of the calibrated methods separately, because it is paid before serving starts.
Over four datasets, it uses 2,000 prompts, 80 settings, and 176,000 generated samples, for a total of 5.73 GPU-hours and 7.16 MJ.
<span id="S6.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online needs no dataset-specific profiling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS8.6" class="ltx_text">VI-H</span> </span><span id="S6.SS8.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Controlled Ablation</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main runs, the remaining budget shrinks over time, so a change in <math id="S6.SS8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> could come from the budget and not from the load.
To separate these effects, we run three versions of <span id="S6.SS8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online:
<span id="S6.SS8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full-HW</span> (live GPU state and online cost learning),
<span id="S6.SS8.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">noHW-State</span> (online cost learning, but the GPU-state input is hidden), and
<span id="S6.SS8.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ReasoningOnly-Strict</span> (no GPU state and online cost learning frozen).
In this experiment we use a large, non-binding energy budget and keep the remaining-energy value that the controller sees fixed.
So the budget cannot drive the decision.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Evaluation</span></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We organize the results around seven questions:
<span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1:</span> Does <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online keep quality and serve every query under changing load?
<span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q2:</span> How does each controller react as load rises, and why does the offline mode defer?
<span id="S7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q3:</span> What does each mode cost in energy, including profiling?
<span id="S7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q4:</span> Is the adaptation really caused by the resource signals?
<span id="S7.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q5:</span> What is the runtime overhead of online control?
<span id="S7.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q6:</span> When is offline calibration the better choice?
<span id="S7.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q7:</span> Does the behavior hold on another GPU and another model?</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.25519v1/figures/fig_per_load.png" id="S7.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/195;" width="476" height="195" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Behavior at each load level (125 queries per level). Top: accuracy over all queries (deferred = wrong). Middle: deferred queries (%). Bottom: mean <math id="S7.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> over <em id="S7.F4.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">answered</em> queries. <span id="S7.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online (blue, solid) never defers and lowers its budget as load rises. <span id="S7.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated (orange, dashed) also lowers its budget, but it starts deferring at medium or high load on all four datasets, which pulls its accuracy down.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span> </span><span id="S7.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Q1: Overall Results under Changing Load</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ VI-D Controlled GPU Load ‣ VI Experimental Methodology ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> gives the main results.
Three points stand out.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST<span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Online serves every query.</span></span>
It has zero deferred queries on all four datasets and stays within the energy budget on all of them (budget used 92–100%).
Every other method defers queries on all four datasets, and Fixed-4 and Adaptive-Cal also exceed the budget once each.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST<span id="S7.SS1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Online gives the best or tied-best accuracy.</span></span>
It has the highest accuracy on all four datasets: GSM8K (0.626), MATH (0.426), ARC-Challenge (0.794), and CommonsenseQA (0.716).
Each result comes from one run of 500 queries, so the 95% confidence interval of one accuracy is about <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>4 points; gaps of up to about 3 points, as on GSM8K, MATH, and CommonsenseQA, are ties.
The clear gains are on ARC-Challenge (+7.2 points over <span id="S7.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated), where <span id="S7.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated loses accuracy mainly because it defers 9.0% of the queries, and at high load (§<a href="#S7.SS2" title="VII-B Q2: Behavior as the Load Rises ‣ VII Evaluation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII-B</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST<span id="S7.SS1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Online uses fewer samples.</span></span>
It has the lowest mean <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> on three of four datasets, even though it answers more queries than any other method, because it stops as soon as the vote is stable.
On MATH only Adaptive-Cal is lower, and only because it defers 55.2% of the queries. The two baselines do much worse on the long-answer datasets: Fixed-4 and Adaptive-Cal defer about half of the GSM8K and MATH queries, at every load level (Fig. <a href="#S7.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ VII Evaluation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
Since each query may use only its share of the remaining budget, four samples often do not fit, so these baselines defer queries from the start.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answer to Q1:</span> Yes. Under changing load, <span id="S7.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online answers every query, stays within budget, and gives the best or tied-best accuracy on all four datasets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VII-B</span> </span><span id="S7.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Q2: Behavior as the Load Rises</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S7.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ VII Evaluation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows each method at each load level.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Both <span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> modes use fewer samples when the GPU is busy.</span>
<span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online lowers its mean budget from idle to high load on all four datasets: from 2.09 to 1.00 on GSM8K, 2.10 to 1.00 on MATH, 2.14 to 1.51 on ARC-Challenge, and 3.37 to 2.54 on CommonsenseQA.
<span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated also lowers its budget (for example, from 3.45 to 1.00 on GSM8K).
Fixed-4 always uses <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=4</annotation></semantics></math> for the queries it answers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST<span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-Online keeps serving at high load.</span></span>
On GSM8K at high load, both <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> modes end up at <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
But <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated defers 53 of the 125 queries (42.4%), so its accuracy for this phase falls to 44.0%.
<span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online answers all 125 queries with 60.0% accuracy and uses less energy per query (175.8 J vs. 206.4 J).
On MATH at high load the pattern is the same: <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated defers 26.4% and reaches 39.2%, while <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online defers none and reaches 43.2%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why does <span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated defer?</span>
We see two reasons in the measurements.
<em id="S7.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">First, it spends more early.</em>
At idle on GSM8K, <span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated uses <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=3.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=3.45</annotation></semantics></math> and 124.4 J per query, while <span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online uses <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=2.09" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.09</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=2.09</annotation></semantics></math> and 60.9 J.
This buys higher idle accuracy (68.0% vs. 60.0%), but the budget is shared by the whole run, so less is left for the high-load phase, when each query is much more expensive.
<em id="S7.SS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Second, its fixed cost model can be too pessimistic.</em>
<span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated admits a query only if the predicted cost fits.
On CommonsenseQA it deferred 23 queries although 21% of the energy budget was never used; on ARC-Challenge it deferred 45 queries with 15% unused.
So the budget was not the limit here; the deferrals came from the controller’s own cost predictions.
Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III Motivation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b) shows the root cause: the offline cost model is often far from the real cost under load.
<span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online avoids both problems because it measures every step and corrects its cost model as it goes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A note on measured load.</span>
The injector settings are identical for all methods, but GPU utilization is sampled before each microbatch, so the reading depends on each method’s execution pattern.
On GSM8K and MATH, all methods see the same high-load utilization (about 85%); for <span id="S7.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online on ARC-Challenge and CommonsenseQA the sampled reading at high load is lower (68% and 45%).
We therefore base our main high-load comparison on GSM8K and MATH.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answer to Q2:</span> As load rises, <span id="S7.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online lowers its budget and keeps serving every query. <span id="S7.SS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated also lowers its budget, but its early spending and fixed cost model lead to deferrals at high load.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">VII-C</span> </span><span id="S7.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Q3: Energy Cost, Including Profiling</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Serving energy.</span>
<span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online has the lowest joules per correct answer on GSM8K (191.9 J) and MATH (371.2 J), the two long-answer datasets where the energy budget is tight.
On ARC-Challenge the two modes are almost equal (19.4 J vs. 19.2 J).
On CommonsenseQA, <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated is more efficient (13.3 J vs. 16.2 J, Table <a href="#S6.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ VI-D Controlled GPU Load ‣ VI Experimental Methodology ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>), but it leaves 4.6% of the queries unanswered.
So <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online is not always the cheapest per correct answer during serving, but it wins the overall trade-off: it gives higher accuracy, answers every query, and, once profiling is counted, uses far less total energy on every dataset.</p>
</div>
