<div id="S7.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main runs, the energy budget shrinks over time.
So the end-to-end results alone cannot tell us whether <span id="S7.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online lowers <math id="S7.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> because of the load or because the budget is running out.
The controlled ablation (§<a href="#S6.SS8" title="VI-H Controlled Ablation ‣ VI Experimental Methodology ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-H</span></a>) removes this confound: the budget is large enough never to bind, and the remaining-energy value seen by the controller is fixed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S7.T5" title="TABLE V ‣ VII-D Q4: Is the Adaptation Caused by Resource Signals? ‣ VII Evaluation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> shows the result.
When both the GPU state and online cost learning are removed (ReasoningOnly-Strict), the controller uses the same mean budget, 3.12 samples per query at every load level on all four datasets.
Its reasoning signal still changes from query to query, but without resource feedback its decisions do not react to the load at all.
When online cost learning is on (noHW-State), the budget changes with the load even though the GPU-state input is hidden.
This happens because the measured energy of each step already reflects the load.
Adding the GPU state (Full-HW) changes the budgets again, most visibly on GSM8K and MATH.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Resource awareness does not mean that <math id="S7.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> must always go down when load goes up.
The decision also depends on how stable the vote is.
What the ablation shows is that removing the resource signals removes the controller’s response to the load.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answer to Q4:</span> Yes. With budget effects removed, online cost learning is necessary for the budget to follow the load, and the GPU state further changes the decisions.</p>
</div>
</section>
