<div id="S7.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Latency and throughput.</span>
Online control is not free (Table <a href="#S7.T6" title="TABLE VI ‣ VII-E Q5: Runtime Overhead and Cost-Model Error ‣ VII Evaluation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>).
<span id="S7.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online has higher p95 latency on three of four datasets, for example 1.08 s vs. 0.51 s on GSM8K and 0.18 s vs. 0.09 s on CommonsenseQA.
Its throughput is about the same on GSM8K, higher on MATH, and lower on the two multiple-choice datasets, most clearly on CommonsenseQA (16.6 vs. 29.7 QPS).
The reason is the step-by-step execution: <span id="S7.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online may run several small microbatches with a decision between them, instead of one batch with all samples.
For short answers, the fixed cost of each step is large compared with the generation time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Online cost-model error.</span>
The online cost model is updated after every step (671 to 993 updates per run).
On the long-answer datasets its energy MAPE is moderate: 23% on GSM8K and 22% on MATH.
On the short multiple-choice datasets it is much higher (134% on ARC-Challenge and 112% on CommonsenseQA).
One likely reason is that a step there uses only a few joules, so small absolute errors become large relative errors.
Even with this error, <span id="S7.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online never exceeded the energy budget.
Two design choices help here: the safety margin <math id="S7.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\sigma_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\sigma_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S5.E8" title="In V-B ATEST-Online ‣ V ATESTDesign ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), and the small step size, which limits the damage of one wrong prediction to one step.
A better cost model for very short steps is future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answer to Q5:</span> The price of online control is higher tail latency and lower throughput on short tasks. The online cost model is accurate enough on long tasks and noisy on short ones, but the controller stays budget-safe.</p>
</div>
