<div id="S7.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Offline calibration is a good choice when the GPU load is stable.
In the idle phase of the load schedule, <span id="S7.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated matches or beats <span id="S7.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online in accuracy on all four datasets (for example, 68.0% vs. 60.0% on GSM8K and 42.4% vs. 39.2% on MATH).
Table <a href="#S7.T8" title="TABLE VIII ‣ VII-F Q6: When Does Offline Calibration Win? ‣ VII Evaluation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VIII</span></a> shows a separate run with no injected load at all.
Here <span id="S7.SS6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated has higher accuracy than <span id="S7.SS6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online on GSM8K (0.686 vs. 0.632), and they are close on the other datasets.
Even Fixed-4 is strong in this setting: it has the lowest J/correct on three of four datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answer to Q6:</span> When the load is stable and the profile matches the runtime, offline calibration, or even a well-chosen fixed budget, is hard to beat. The advantage of <span id="S7.SS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online appears when the load changes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS7.6" class="ltx_text">VII-G</span> </span><span id="S7.SS7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Q7: Does the Behavior Hold on Another GPU and Another Model?</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We repeat the experiment on a second GPU, an NVIDIA L40S, with Phi-3-mini and Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct on all four datasets.
Everything else follows the A100 protocol; only <math id="S7.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is re-measured, because the cost of a sample depends on the GPU and the model.
We compare Fixed-4 with <span id="S7.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online; <span id="S7.SS7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated and Adaptive-Cal would first need a new offline profiling of every dataset on this GPU, which is exactly the cost that <span id="S7.SS7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online avoids.
Table <a href="#S7.T7" title="TABLE VII ‣ VII-E Q5: Runtime Overhead and Cost-Model Error ‣ VII Evaluation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a> shows the results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zero deferrals.</span>
<span id="S7.SS7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online answers every query in all eight new settings within the energy budget (92–99.5% used), while Fixed-4 defers up to 48.8%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy.</span>
With Phi-3-mini, <span id="S7.SS7.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online reaches almost the same accuracy on the L40S as on the A100 (0.638 vs. 0.626 on GSM8K and 0.448 vs. 0.426 on MATH) and keeps its gains over Fixed-4: +13.2 points on GSM8K, +6.8 on MATH, and +12.0 on ARC-Challenge.
With Qwen2.5-3B, it is more accurate than Fixed-4 on every dataset: it doubles GSM8K accuracy (0.138 to 0.280) and raises MATH from 0.242 to 0.348, ARC-Challenge from 0.380 to 0.488, and CommonsenseQA from 0.414 to 0.564.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Energy.</span>
<span id="S7.SS7.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online lowers joules per correct answer in seven of eight settings.
With Phi-3-mini on the L40S, the drop is 22% on GSM8K (303.0 vs. 386.2 J), 16% on MATH, and 15% on ARC-Challenge; with Qwen2.5-3B it is larger, from 21% on ARC-Challenge to almost half on GSM8K (536 vs. 1019 J).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">When the budget is loose.</span>
The one exception is CommonsenseQA with Phi-3-mini on the L40S.
Here the budget is loose enough for Fixed-4 to answer every query with <math id="S7.SS7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=4</annotation></semantics></math>, and the two methods are almost equal (0.715 vs. 0.718 accuracy, 17.7 vs. 17.9 J).
This agrees with Q6: online control helps most when the budget is tight.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS7.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answer to Q7:</span> Yes. On a second GPU and a second model, <span id="S7.SS7.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online again answers every query within the budget, is at least as accurate as Fixed-4 in all eight settings, and uses less energy per correct answer in seven of them.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VIII </span><span id="S8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Discussion</span></h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Which mode should be used?</span>
If the model, GPU, and workload stay the same and the load is stable, the profile of <span id="S8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated can be built once and reused, giving strong accuracy, low tail latency, and high throughput.
If the GPU is shared, the load changes, or new datasets and models arrive often, <span id="S8.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online is the safer choice: it needs no profiling, follows the live cost, and serves every query.
The two modes can also be combined by starting <span id="S8.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online from a calibrated cost model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Service coverage is a systems metric.</span>
A controller can look good on the queries it answers while refusing many others, so we count deferred queries as wrong.
On GSM8K at high load, this shows the real difference: 44.0% accuracy with 42.4% deferrals for <span id="S8.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated, against 60.0% with none for <span id="S8.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Resource awareness is more than “use fewer samples when busy.”</span>
<span id="S8.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online compares the value of the next step with its current cost: it stops on a stable vote even on an idle GPU, and continues on a split vote even under load if the cost is acceptable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why not let an LLM decide?</span>
A variant in which an LLM makes the continue-or-stop decision reached similar accuracy, but exceeded the energy budget on three of four datasets (by up to 6.2% on GSM8K) and spent extra GPU energy on its decisions.
A small numerical controller is cheaper and easier to keep within a hard budget.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IX </span><span id="S9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Limitations and Future Work</span></h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation scope.</span>
Our evaluation is bounded by the available compute.
Every setting must be run for every method and load level, together with the stable-load runs, the ablations, and the offline calibration, so the total GPU time grows quickly.
We therefore use 500 queries per dataset, four datasets, two open models, and two GPU types, which is enough to compare all methods under the same conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Runtime overhead.</span>
Step-by-step control adds some overhead on short tasks (§<a href="#S7.SS5" title="VII-E Q5: Runtime Overhead and Cost-Model Error ‣ VII Evaluation ‣ ATEST: Adaptive Resource-Aware Test-Time Scaling for LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII-E</span></a>).
Batching the steps of several queries and overlapping decisions with generation could reduce it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Online cost model.</span>
The online cost model of <span id="S9.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online is simple by design.
Richer online models could
make cost predictions more precise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Integration with inference systems.</span>
<span id="S9.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> decides how much reasoning each query gets, while inference engines and power managers decide how that work runs.
Running <span id="S9.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span> inside engines such as vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> or with GPU frequency control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> could manage both together.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">X </span><span id="S10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cost of a test-time scaling sample is not fixed on a shared GPU; in our measurements it changes by up to 4.1<math id="S10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> with load.
We presented <span id="S10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>, which makes the sampling decision depend on both the value of more reasoning and its current cost.
Its default mode, <span id="S10.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online, learns the cost online, decides step by step, and needs no profiling; its offline mode, <span id="S10.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Calibrated, works well when the load is stable and one profile can be reused.
Under changing load on four datasets, <span id="S10.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ATEST</span>-Online answered every query, stayed within the energy budget, and gave the highest or tied-best accuracy, while the offline mode deferred up to 42.4% of queries at high load. The same behavior held on an L40S GPU and with a second model, Qwen2.5-3B.
It also avoided 5.73 GPU-hours of offline calibration.
A controlled ablation showed that online cost learning is what makes the budget follow the load.
The price is higher tail latency and lower throughput on short tasks.
Our results suggest that test-time scaling on shared accelerators should be treated as a runtime resource-management problem, not only as a reasoning problem.</p>
</div>
</section>
